On Friday, March 31, 2017 at 9:47:17 AM UTC+11, Joe Cheng [RStudio] wrote:
> The `output$textbox <- ...` should not be inside the observeEvent. The video "Effective Reactive Programming Part 1" on this page <https://www.rstudio.com/resources/webinars/shiny-developer-conference/> goes into detail as to why.
>
> isolate({...}) does NOT mean "withholds executing the logic if the action button is 0". Instead, it means that any reactive values/expressions that are accessed inside the isolate(), should NOT be listened to for changes. So for example:
>
> output$textbox <- renderText({
>   input$variableNumericInput
> })
>
> output$textbox would update automatically every time input$variableNumericInput changes. If instead you do this:
>
> output$textbox <- renderText({
>   isolate(input$variableNumericInput)
> })
>
> then textbox will only ever show the initial value of input$variableNumericInput; it won't change. If you want to update on button press:
>
> output$textbox <- renderText({
>   req(input$goButtonCustom)
>   isolate({
>     input$variableNumericInput
>   })
> })
>
> Since input$goButtonCustom appears outside of the isolate(), it will cause the textbox's renderText expression to reexecute. The req() is there to prevent the rest of the code from running if the button has not been clicked. See the article <https://shiny.rstudio.com/articles/req.html> for more.
>
